          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#plain_cobordism_hypothesis Plain cobordism hypothesis
          %p
            What is known as the
            %em Cobordism Hypothesis
            is, in its most basic form, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
            that, roughly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi fr
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_n^{fr}
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+manifold"} framing
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalently
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+construction"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,n)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+with+duals"} with duals
            on a single
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          %p
            The idea is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BaezDolan95"} Baez &amp; Dolan (1995)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              \-tuply monoidal
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (i.e. for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq \infty
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tangle+Hypothesis"} Tangle Hypothesis
            (
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/q-alg/9503002.pdf#page=23"}> p. 23
            ), before definitions of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theoretical
            notions were available. The term “cobordism hypothesis” is used by Baez and Dolan only in passing and without definition (
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/q-alg/9503002.pdf#page=25"}> p. 25
            ) but was popularized by
            %a{:href => "#Lurie09"} Lurie (2009)
            who gave a detailed though still incomplete sketch of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            by appeal to the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+complete+Segal+space"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-fold complete Segal spaces
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+categories"} geometric definition of higher categories
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/18/10/18-10abs.html"} tac:18-10
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definitions+of+higher+categories"} algebraic definitions of higher categories
          %p
            Since a (framed) fully
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+topological+quantum+field+theory"} extended topological quantum field theory
            (TQFT) may be identified with a symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
              \-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi n
                      %mi fr
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \colon Bord^{fr}_n \to \mathcal{C}^\otimes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+extended+TQFTs"} fully extended TQFTs
            are entirely determined by their value on the point (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} n-vector space
            of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+objects"} fully dualizable objects
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{\otimes}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalently
            encode such fully extended TQFTs. As such the hypothesis is actually called the “
            %em> Extended TQFT Hypothesis
            ” by
            %a{:href => "#BaezDolan95"} Baez &amp; Dolan (1995)
            (
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/q-alg/9503002.pdf#page=28"}> p. 28
            ).
          %p
            From the cobordism hypothesis for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+manifold"} framing
            structure it follows fairly readily that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-groupoid
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+objects"} fully dualizable objects
            in a symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
            and that cobordisms with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangential+structure"} tangential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \hookrightarrow O(n)
            are similarly classified by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fixed+points"} homotopy fixed loci
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restricted+action"} corresponding
            = succeed "-action" do
