          %p The idea of the cobordism hypothesis is that:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinders"} cylinders
                count as trivial (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"}> identity
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
                between their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                components, corresponding to how local
                = succeed "-evolution" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
                is trivial (corresponding to the physics jargon that “their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                vanishes”),
            %li
              %p
                so that all the non-trivial information in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                and in
                = succeed "-propagation" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
                = succeed "-attachments" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/handle+body"} handle body
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangle+identity"} zig-zag rules
                %a{:href => "adjunction#InTermsOfStringDiagrams"} known from
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-adjunctions"} 2-adjunctions
                and their further higher dimensional analogs,
            %li
              %p
                which means that the point – regarded as a 0-dimensional element of an
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                – is equipped with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
                of, first of all, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable object
                under the relevant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                of cobordisms (which is
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                = succeed "-dualizable" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq n
          %h3#GeometricCobordismHypothesis Geometric cobordism hypothesis
          %p
            More generally, the
            %em geometric
            cobordism hypothesis &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#GradyPavlov21"}> Grady &amp; Pavlov (2021)
            \&amp;rbrack; asserts that general non-topological
            %em geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+field+theory"} extended functorial field theories
            (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            as well as the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+quantum+field+theory"} relativistic
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} QFT on curved spacetimes
          %p
            For example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS-theorem+on+rational+2d+CFT"} FRS-theorem on rational 2d CFT
            is, at least in spirit, a realization of this idea: It states that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+CFT"} rational
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+conformal+field+theories"} 2d conformal field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebras"} vertex operator algebras
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+nets"} conformal nets
            or similar and (2.) global purely topological data (a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+object"} algebra object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+category"} of representations
            of that
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
          %p Conversely, the plain (topological) cobordism hypothesis is the special case of the geometric cobordism hypothesis of no geometric structure and in this sense very much captures the idea which in physics is known as “TQFTs are those QFTS without local degrees of freedom”.
          %p
            In general, where the notion of extended (geometric)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+field+theory"} functorial field theory
            is one candidate for a definition of (non-topological)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            Notice that the local behaviour of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is still immensely rich: It keeps busy quantum field theorists most of whom never encounter a topological effect in their life. For example the original statement of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            are entirely concerned only with the local behaviour of Lorentzian QFTs (namely on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \geq 4
            remains wide open.
          %p
            In this vein, much in the spirit of
            %a{:href => "category+theory#WhatIsTriviallyTrivial"} Freyd’s dictum
            on the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
            in general, one may read the (geometric) cobordism hypothesis as trivializing the trivial part of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            A proof of the geometric cobordism hypothesis is thus the important first step towards the
            %a{:href => "mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} currently wide-open and yet crucial problem
            of mathematically constructing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} non-perturbative quantum field theories
          %h2#formalization Formalization
          %p
            In &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Lurie09"}> Lurie 2009
            \&amp;rbrack; a sketch of an approach to the formalization and proof of the cobordism hypothesis is described.
