                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %h3#ForCobordismsWithSingularities For cobordisms with singularities (boundaries/branes and defects/domain walls)
          %p
            There is a vast generalization of the plain
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            to be equipped with various types of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie 09, Definition Sketch 4.3.2
            ).
          %p
            Each type of singularity in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            now corresponds to a new generator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie 09, Theorem 4.3.11
          %p
            For instance if the generator on top of the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphism"} 1-morphism
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to \ast
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with all dual are equivalent to choices of morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
            is the fully dualizable object assigned to the point, as before, and now equipped with a morphism from the tensor unit into it. Indeed, this is the usual datum that describes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            in QFT (see for instance at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} QFT with defects
          %h3#ForNoncompactCobordisms For noncompact cobordisms
          %p
            One important variant of the category of cobordisms is obtained by discarding all those morphisms which have non-empty incoming (say, dually one could use outgoing) boundary component. Then a representation of this category imposes on its values “cups but no caps”, hence only half of the data of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable object
            in the given degree.
          %p
            Accordingly, in this case the cobordism hypothesis says that such a functor is given not quite by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+object"} fully dualizable object
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+object"} Calabi-Yau object
            (see there for more).
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, section 4.2
            )
          %p
            2-dimensional TQFT of this form is known as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
          %h3#for_cobordisms_with_geometric_structure For cobordisms with geometric structure
          %p
            A non-topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is a representation of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
            for cobordisms equipped with extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} stuff, structure, property
            that is “not just topological”, meaning roughly not of the form of def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CatOfCobordismsWithXIStructure"}
          %p The theory for this more general case is not as far developed yet.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                steps towards classification of quantum field theories with
                %em super-Euclidean structure
                are discussed at
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%281%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+K-theory"} (1,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and K-theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-dimensional+Euclidean+field+theories+and+tmf"} (2,1)-dimensional Euclidean field theories and tmf
            %li
              %p
                a general definition of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
                of cobordisms equipped with
                %strong geometric structure
                given by a morphism into, roughly, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth infinity-groupoid
                of structure is discussed in (
                %a{:href => "#Ayala"}> Ayala
                ).
          %p
            A full-blown geometric cobordism statement is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GradyPavlov21"} Grady &amp; Pavlov 2021
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %h3#morphisms_of_tqfts Morphisms of TQFTs
          %p
            In particular this means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Fun
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi fr
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun^\otimes(Bord_n^{fr} , C )
            is itself an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infinity,0)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %p
            When interpreting symmetric monoidal functors from bordisms to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %p
            According to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Schommer-Pries"} Chris Schommer-Pries
            interesting morphisms of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} QFT with defects
            \.
          %h3#invariants_determined_from_the_point Invariants determined from the point
          %p
            The theorem does say that the invariant attached by an extended TQFT to the point determines all the higher invariants – however it is important to notice that there are strong constraints on what is assigned to the point. For an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em framed
            theory one needs to assign a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+object"} fully dualizable object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            For an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
          %p
            In the 1d case, this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(1)
            action on dualizable objects takes every object to its dual, and an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(1)
            fixed point is indeed a vector space with a nondegenerate symmetric inner product.
          %p
            For an
            %em oriented
            theory
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-fixed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            is nothing but for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            ends up meaning a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+category"} Calabi-Yau category
            (in the case the target 2-category is that of categories).
          %p
            In fact something more general is true: if one wants a theory that takes values on manifolds equipped with a
            = succeed "-structure," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            any group mapping to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
            (such as for instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            already discussed or its higher versions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+structure"} String structure
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fivebrane+structure"} Fivebrane structure
            or …) one needs to assign to the point a
            = succeed "-fixed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            acting through
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
          %p
            This beautifully includes all the above plus for example
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            – here we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (mapping trivially to
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            involves a map to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}G \simeq X
          %p
            Such theories are classified by
            = succeed "-families" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Notice that there is an important subtlety of Lurie’s theorem in the case of manifolds with
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-fixed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %em homotopy
            fixed points. This is very important because a homotopy fixed point is not just a property. It is additional structure. Depending on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            One can see this in the 1 dimensional case: there is no property of vector spaces which automatically endows them with an inner product, but it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} extra structure
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
